      "claim": "Doxazosin oral bioavailability was 65 ± 14%, above the threshold.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C30"],
      "claim": "Meperidine was listed as low oral bioavailability in a table, but this is contradicted by direct absolute bioavailability measurements of 0.48–0.80.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C07"],
      "claim": "Encainide low bioavailability was noted in some patients, but quantitative absolute bioavailability studies show mean values of 30–42%, above the threshold.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "All high-transfer experimental evidence from diverse drugs reports absolute oral bioavailability values above the 20% threshold, mostly 24–88%. The only qualitative 'low' reports for meperidine and encainide are contradicted by direct quantitative absolute bioavailability measurements. Therefore the query is predicted to have high oral bioavailability at the F ≥ 20% threshold.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct experimental bioavailability data for the query molecule itself.",
    "No structurally close analogue with a measured low oral bioavailability was retrieved.",
    "The query's potential first-pass metabolism liability is not directly captured by the retrieved evidence."
  ],
